To correctly calculate the **Normalized Linear Approximation Table (NLAT)** in Java, we must use bitwise operations to compute the dot product (parity of the bitwise AND) between the input/output masks and the values. The discrepancy often arises from incorrect parity calculation or improper normalization.

The formula for each entry $(a, b)$ in the table is:
$$NLAT(a, b) = \#\{x \in \{0, \dots, 7\} \mid \text{parity}(a \cdot x) = \text{parity}(b \cdot S(x))\} - 4$$

### Java Implementation

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class SBoxLAT {
    // The S-box provided in the context (3-bit permutation)
    private static final int[] S_BOX = {3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 1, 2, 0};
    private static final int N = 8; // 2^3 for a 3-bit S-box

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("S-box: " + Arrays.toString(S_BOX));
        int[][] lat = calculateNLAT(S_BOX);
        printTable(lat);
        validate(lat);
    }

    /**
     * Calculates the NLAT entries using the bitwise dot product parity.
     */
    public static int[][] calculateNLAT(int[] sbox) {
        int[][] lat = new int[N][N];

        for (int a = 0; a < N; a++) { // Input mask
            for (int b = 0; b < N; b++) { // Output mask
                int matches = 0;
                for (int x = 0; x < N; x++) {
                    // Dot product: parity of (mask AND value)
                    int inputParity = Integer.bitCount(a & x) % 2;
                    int outputParity = Integer.bitCount(b & sbox[x]) % 2;

                    if (inputParity == outputParity) {
                        matches++;
                    }
                }
                // Normalization: count - 2^(n-1)
                lat[a][b] = matches - (N / 2);
            }
